helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Using Procedures and Functions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149456				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>procedures\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;passing to procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;for procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;passing by reference or value\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;scope\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>scope of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>GLOBAL variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PUBLIC variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PRIVATE variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;return value type\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>return value type of functions\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\"\>Using Procedures and Functions\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767				0	ca-XR	The following describes the basic use of procedures and functions in $[officename] Basic.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151215				0	ca-XR	When you create a new module, $[officename] Basic automatically inserts a SUB called "Main". This default name has nothing to do with the order or the starting point of a $[officename] Basic project. You can also safely rename this SUB.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id314756320				0	ca-XR	Some restrictions apply for the names of your public variables, subs, and functions. You must not use the same name as one of the modules of the same library.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124				0	ca-XR	Procedures (SUBS) and functions (FUNCTIONS) help you maintaining a structured overview by separating a program into logical pieces.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	ca-XR	One benefit of procedures and functions is that, once you have developed a program code containing task components, you can use this code in another project.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	ca-XR	Passing Variables to Procedures (SUB) and Functions (FUNCTION)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414				0	ca-XR	Variables can be passed to both procedures and functions. The SUB or FUNCTION must be declared to expect parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	ca-XR	SUB SubName(\<emph\>Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...\</emph\>)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151114				0	ca-XR	Program code				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	ca-XR	END SUB				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	ca-XR	The SUB is called using the following syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	ca-XR	SubName(Value1, Value2,...)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124				0	ca-XR	The parameters passed to a SUB must fit to those specified in the SUB declaration.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147397				0	ca-XR	The same process applies to FUNCTIONS. In addition, functions always return a function result. The result of a function is defined by assigning the return value to the function name:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	ca-XR	FUNCTION FunctionName(Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...) As Type				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	ca-XR	Program code				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	ca-XR	\<emph\>FunctionName=Result\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	ca-XR	End Function				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839				0	ca-XR	The FUNCTION is called using the following syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146914				0	ca-XR	Variable=FunctionName(Parameter1, Parameter2,...)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B3				0	ca-XR	You can also use the fully qualified name to call a procedure or function:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Library.Module.Macro()\</item\>\<br/\> For example, to call the Autotext macro from the Gimmicks library, use the following command:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Gimmicks.AutoText.Main()\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156276				0	ca-XR	Passing Variables by Value or Reference				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155765				0	ca-XR	Parameters can be passed to a SUB or a FUNCTION either by reference or by value. Unless otherwise specified, a parameter is always passed by reference. That means that a SUB or a FUNCTION gets the parameter and can read and modify its value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	ca-XR	If you want to pass a parameter by value insert the key word "ByVal" in front of the parameter when you call a SUB or FUNCTION, for example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042				0	ca-XR	Result = Function(\<emph\>ByVal\</emph\> Parameter)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258				0	ca-XR	In this case, the original content of the parameter will not be modified by the FUNCTION since it only gets the value and not the parameter itself.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150982				0	ca-XR	Scope of Variables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149814				0	ca-XR	A variable defined within a SUB or FUNCTION, only remains valid until the procedure is exited. This is known as a "local" variable. In many cases, you need a variable to be valid in all procedures, in every module of all libraries, or after a SUB or FUNCTION is exited.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154186				0	ca-XR	Declaring Variables Outside a SUB or FUNCTION				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150208				0	ca-XR	GLOBAL VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145258				0	ca-XR	The variable is valid as long as the $[officename] session lasts.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198				0	ca-XR	PUBLIC VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	ca-XR	The variable is valid in all modules.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3158212				0	ca-XR	PRIVATE VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152994				0	ca-XR	The variable is only valid in this module.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150886				0	ca-XR	DIM VarName As TYPENAME				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150368				0	ca-XR	The variable is only valid in this module.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id5097506				0	ca-XR	Example for private variables				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id8738975				0	ca-XR	Enforce private variables to be private across modules by setting CompatibilityMode(true).				20200411 15:09:53
