helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149523				0	ca-XR	To query Yes/No fields, use the following syntax for dBASE tables:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153180				0	ca-XR	Status				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147481				0	ca-XR	Query criterion				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155187				0	ca-XR	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156092				0	ca-XR	Yes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152414				0	ca-XR	for dBASE tables: not equal to any given value				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151265				0	ca-XR	=1 returns all records where the Yes/No field has the status "Yes" or "On" (selected in black),				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152450				0	ca-XR	No				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150997				0	ca-XR	.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155331				0	ca-XR	=0 returns all records for which the Yes/No field has the status "No" or "Off" (no selection).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154179				0	ca-XR	Null				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147035				0	ca-XR	IS NULL				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159385				0	ca-XR	IS NULL returns all records for which the Yes/No field has neither of the states Yes or No (selected in gray).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157888				0	ca-XR	The syntax depends on the database system used. You should also note that Yes/No fields can be defined differently (only 2 states instead of 3).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145772				0	ca-XR	Parameter queries				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157312				0	ca-XR	You must place the variable between square brackets (=[x]) to create a query with variable parameters. Alternatively, you can use an equal sign followed by a colon (=:x). When the query is executed, the program will display a dialog asking you for the expression to which the variable x should be assigned.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150818				0	ca-XR	If you query several parameters at the same time, you will see a list field in the dialog containing all of the parameters and an input line alongside each one. Enter the values, preferably from top to bottom, and press the Enter key after each line.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157855				0	ca-XR	Parameter queries with placeholders (*, _) or special characters (for example, ?) are not possible.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157537				0	ca-XR	If you formulate a parameter query and you save it with the variables, you can later create a query in which only the variables have to be replaced by the expressions that you want. $[officename] asks for these variables in a dialog as soon as you open the query.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151035				0	ca-XR	Parameter Input				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153596				0	ca-XR	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>The \<emph\>Parameter Input\</emph\> dialog asks you which variables you defined in the query. Enter a value for each query variable and confirm by clicking \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150585				0	ca-XR	Parameter queries are also used for \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170203.xhp\" name=\"subforms\"\>subforms\</link\>, since they work exclusively with queries for which the values to be invoked are read internally from a variable.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153645				0	ca-XR	A parameter query can have the following form in an SQL statement:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156731				0	ca-XR	select * from 'addresses' where 'name' = :placeholder				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145181				0	ca-XR	SQL Mode				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147013				0	ca-XR	SQL stands for "Structured Query Language" and describes instructions for updating and administering relational databases.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152570	136			0	ca-XR	In $[officename] you do not need any knowledge of SQL for most queries, since you do not have to enter the SQL code. If you create a query in the query design, $[officename] automatically converts your instructions into the corresponding SQL syntax. If, with the help of the \<emph\>Switch Design View On/Off \</emph\>button, you change to the SQL view, you can see the SQL commands for a query that has been created previously.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152412				0	ca-XR	You can formulate your query directly in the SQL code. Note, however, that the special syntax is dependent upon the database system that you use.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146842				0	ca-XR	If you enter the SQL code manually, you can create SQL-specific queries that are not supported by the graphical interface in \<emph\>Query design\</emph\>. These queries must be executed in native SQL mode.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149632				0	ca-XR	By clicking the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14030000.xhp\" name=\"Run SQL command directly\"\>\<emph\>Run SQL command directly\</emph\>\</link\> icon in the SQL view, you can formulate a query that is not processed by $[officename].				20200411 15:09:53
