helpcontent2	source\text\smath\04\01020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145249				0	ca-XR	Move left or right to the next category or function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\04\01020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150928				0	ca-XR	Enter key				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\04\01020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148390				0	ca-XR	Selects a category (within the category section) or inserts a function in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window (within the function section).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\04\01020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155988				0	ca-XR	Tab				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\04\01020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153723				0	ca-XR	Jump from the first category item to the first function of the category.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\04\01020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3083451				0	ca-XR	Shift+Tab				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\04\01020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151181				0	ca-XR	Jump from the last category item to the last function of the category.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Inserting Brackets				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153415				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; inserting in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;brackets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>distances between brackets\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153415				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"parentheses\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/parentheses.xhp\" name=\"Inserting Brackets\"\>Inserting Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150751				0	ca-XR	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Math, can brackets be shown separately so that the distance between them is freely definable?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3083281				0	ca-XR	You can set individual brackets using "left" and "right", but the distance between the brackets will not be fixed, as they adapt to the argument. Nevertheless, there is a way to display brackets so that the distance between them is fixed. To accomplish this, place a "\\" (backslash) before the normal brackets. These brackets now behave like any other symbol and the alignment is the same as with other symbols:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3155960				0	ca-XR	left lbrace x right none				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3148489				0	ca-XR	size *2 langle x rangle				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3150344				0	ca-XR	size *2 \\langle x \\rangle				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Working with Limits				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	bm_id8404492				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>limits;in sums/integrals\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>integral limits\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id1892147				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"limits\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/limits.xhp\"\>Working with Limits\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	hd_id9881893				0	ca-XR	How can I define the limits in a Sum or Integral formula?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6504409				0	ca-XR	You want to insert a summation formula like "summation of s^k from k = 0 to n" at the cursor in a Writer text document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8811304				0	ca-XR	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1276589				0	ca-XR	You see the Math input window and the Elements window. If you don't see the Elements window, you can enable it in the View menu.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3283791				0	ca-XR	In the upper part of the Elements window, click the \<emph\>Operators\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9734794				0	ca-XR	In the lower part of the Elements window, click the \<emph\>Sum\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9641712				0	ca-XR	To enable lower and upper limits, click additionally the \<emph\>Upper and Lower Limits\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3304239				0	ca-XR	In the input window, the first placeholder or marker is selected, and you can start to enter the lower limit: 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8679158				0	ca-XR	k = 0				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id8471327				0	ca-XR	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the upper limit:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4731894				0	ca-XR	n				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id759300				0	ca-XR	Press F4 to advance to the next marker, and enter the summand:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id651875				0	ca-XR	s^k				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id6756187				0	ca-XR	Now the formula is complete. Click into your text document outside the formula to leave the formula editor.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9406414				0	ca-XR	In the same way, you can enter an Integral formula with limits. When you click an icon from the Elements window, the assigned text command is inserted in the input window. If you know the text commands, you can enter the commands directly in the input window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id5866267				0	ca-XR	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Object - Formula\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id1965697				0	ca-XR	Click in the input window and enter the following line:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id9004486				0	ca-XR	int from{a} to{b} f(x)`dx				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id4651020				0	ca-XR	A small gap exists between f(x) and dx, which you can also enter using the Elements window: click the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> icon, then the \<emph\>Small Gap\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3877071				0	ca-XR	If you don't like the font of the letters f and x, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Fonts\</item\> and select other fonts. Click the \<emph\>Default\</emph\> button to use the new fonts as default from now on.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id3021332				0	ca-XR	If you need the formula within a line of text, the limits increase the line height. You can choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Text Mode\</item\> to place the limits besides the Sum or Integral symbol, which reduces the line height.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\limits.xhp	0	help	par_id260322				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03090909.xhp\"\>Example of Integral and Sum ranges\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Merging Formula Parts in Brackets				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	bm_id3152596				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; merging formula parts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; merging\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>fractions in formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>merging;formula parts\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152596				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"brackets\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/brackets.xhp\" name=\"Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\"\>Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154511				0	ca-XR	Inserting fractions into formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971				0	ca-XR	In the case of a fraction whose numerator and denominator consist of a product, a sum, and so on, the values that belong together must be bracketed together.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3149021				0	ca-XR	Use the following syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3083280				0	ca-XR	{a + c} over 2 = m				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3154703				0	ca-XR	or				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3150297				0	ca-XR	m = {a + c} over 2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Instructions for Using $[officename] Math				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147341				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>$[officename] Math;general instructions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>instructions; $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Equation Editor, see $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147341				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"main\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/main.xhp\" name=\"Instructions for Using $[officename] Math\"\>Instructions for Using $[officename] Math\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\main.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150199				0	ca-XR	Entering and Editing Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Entering Line Breaks				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	bm_id1295205				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>line breaks; in formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas;line breaks\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>wrapping text;in formulas\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146970				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"newline\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/newline.xhp\" name=\"Entering Line Breaks\"\>Entering Line Breaks\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147339				0	ca-XR	How to write formulas in $[officename] Math over two lines (with manual line break):				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	par_id3154702				0	ca-XR	Create a line break by using the "newline" command. Everything coming after the line break is placed on the next line.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Entering Comments				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155961				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>comments; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;comments in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155961				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"comment\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/comment.xhp\" name=\"Entering Comments\"\>Entering Comments\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657				0	ca-XR	How does one attach comments that don't appear in the document to a formula?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_id3149499				0	ca-XR	A comment begins with a double percent sign \<emph\>%%\</emph\>, and extends to the next line-end character (Enter key). Everything that lies in between is ignored and is not printed out. If there are percent signs in the text, they are treated as part of the text.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	ca-XR	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\comment.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D3				0	ca-XR	a^2+b^2=c^2 %% Pythagorean theorem.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Manually Aligning Formula Parts				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156384				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>aligning; characters in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formula parts; manually aligning\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156384				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"align\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/align.xhp\" name=\"Manually Aligning Formula Parts\"\>Manually Aligning Formula Parts\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657				0	ca-XR	How do you align characters in $[officename] Math quickly and easily?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3150249				0	ca-XR	To accomplish this, you must define empty groups and character strings. They do not require any space, but carry information that helps in the alignment process.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912				0	ca-XR	To create empty groups, enter curly brackets \<emph\>{}\</emph\> in the Commands window. In the following example, the goal is to achieve a line break so that the plus signs are beneath one another, even though one less character is entered in the upper line:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246				0	ca-XR	a+a+a+{} newline {}{}{}{}{}a+a+a+a				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3143229				0	ca-XR	Empty character strings are a simple way to ensure that texts and formulas are left-aligned. They are defined using double inverted commas "" . Make sure you do not use any typographic inverted commas. Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153809				0	ca-XR	"A further example." newline a+b newline ""c-d				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149018				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>accessibility; $[officename] Math shortcuts\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149018				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"keyboard\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/keyboard.xhp\" name=\"Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\"\>Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150298				0	ca-XR	You can control $[officename] Math without a mouse.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150343				0	ca-XR	Inserting a Formula Directly				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659				0	ca-XR	If you want to insert a formula into a text document, and you already know the correct writing, you can proceed as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153818				0	ca-XR	Write the formula into your text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153915				0	ca-XR	Select the formula				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213				0	ca-XR	Choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154767				0	ca-XR	Inserting a Formula using a Window				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875				0	ca-XR	If you want to use the $[officename] Math interface to edit a formula, choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\> without any text selected.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391				0	ca-XR	The cursor waits in the Commands window and you can type the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150537				0	ca-XR	You can compose formulas using the Elements window. Open it with the menu \<emph\>View - Elements\</emph\> if it is not already open.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3155625				0	ca-XR	If the Elements window is open, use F6 to switch from the Commands window to the Elements window and back.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154554				0	ca-XR	Elements window				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Changing Default Attributes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145792				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; changing in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>font attributes;changing defaults\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;changing default attributes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defaults;changing default formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing;default formatting\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145792				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"attributes\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/attributes.xhp\" name=\"Changing Default Attributes\"\>Changing Default Attributes\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154484				0	ca-XR	Can default formats in $[officename] Math be modified?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3148870				0	ca-XR	Some parts of formulas are always formatted bold or italic by default.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210				0	ca-XR	You can remove these attributes using "nbold" and "nitalic". Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3143231				0	ca-XR	a + b				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810				0	ca-XR	nitalic a + bold b.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872				0	ca-XR	In the second formula, the a is not italic. The b is bold. You cannot change the plus sign by this method.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Entering Text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155962				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>text strings; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>direct text; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;text in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id5676442				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"text\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/text.xhp\" name=\"Entering Text\"\>Entering Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id8509170				0	ca-XR	How to enter direct text strings that do not get interpreted?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id337229				0	ca-XR	Some text strings get interpreted as operators automatically. Sometimes this is not what you want. If you want to write W\<emph\>*\</emph\> (a letter with a superscripted asterisk), the asterisk will be interpreted as a multiplication operator. Enclose the direct text within double quotes or add spaceholders.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	ca-XR	Examples:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id521866				0	ca-XR	An imported MathType formula contains the following string				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9413739				0	ca-XR	W rSup { size 8{*} }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id755943				0	ca-XR	If you have set up Math to convert imported MathType formulas (in \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Load/Save - Microsoft Office), you see the formula with a placeholder instead of the asterisk.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id5988952				0	ca-XR	Change {*} to {} * {} as in the following formula:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id3304544				0	ca-XR	W rSup { size 8{} * {} }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id4941557				0	ca-XR	You can also use W^"*" to enter the character as direct text.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9961851				0	ca-XR	Some formulas start with an = sign. Use "=" to enter that character as direct text.				20200411 15:09:53
