helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154715				0	ca-XR	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SMA_TOX\"\>Inserts the range statement \<emph\>upper limit\</emph\> for integral and summation with placeholders\</ahelp\> You can also type \<emph\>to <?><?>\</emph\> directly in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window. Limit statements can only be used if combined with the appropriate operators.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149233				0	ca-XR	You can also add limits to an operator (for example, an integral) by first clicking the desired operator and then clicking the \<emph\>limit\</emph\> symbol. This method is faster than typing the commands directly.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155076				0	ca-XR	The command \<emph\>liminf\</emph\> inserts the \<emph\>limit inferior\</emph\> with one placeholder.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154323				0	ca-XR	The command \<emph\>limsup\</emph\> inserts the \<emph\>limit superior\</emph\> with one placeholder.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146956				0	ca-XR	By typing \<emph\>oper\</emph\> in the Commands window, you can insert \<emph\>user-defined operators\</emph\> in $[officename] Math, a feature useful for incorporating special characters into a formula. An example is \<emph\>oper %theta x\</emph\>. Using the \<emph\>oper\</emph\> command, you can also insert characters not in the default $[officename] character set. \<emph\>oper\</emph\> can also be used in connection with limits; for example, \<emph\>oper %union from {i=1} to n x_{i}\</emph\>. In this example, the union symbol is indicated by the name \<emph\>union\</emph\>. However, this is not one of the predefined symbols. To define it, choose \<emph\>Tools - Catalog\</emph\>. select \<emph\>Special\</emph\> as the symbol set in the dialog that appears, then click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button. In the next dialog, select \<emph\>Special\</emph\> as the symbol set again. Enter a meaningful name in the \<emph\>Symbol\</emph\> text box, for example, "union" and then click the union symbol in the set of symbols. Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\> and then \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Close\</emph\> to close the \<emph\>Symbols\</emph\> dialog. You are now finished and can type the union symbol in the Commands window, by entering \<emph\>oper %union\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154243				0	ca-XR	Limits can be arranged in ways other than centered above/below the operator. Use the options provided by $[officename] Math for working with superscript and subscript indexes. For example, type \<emph\>sum_a^b c\</emph\> in the Commands window to arrange the limits to the right of the sum symbol. If your limit entries contain longer expressions, you must put them in group brackets, for example, sum_{i=1}^{2*n} b. When formulas are imported from older versions this is done automatically. To change the spacing (gaps) between the characters choose \<emph\>Format - Spacing - Category - \</emph\>\<link href=\"text/smath/01/05030000.xhp\" name=\"Indexes\"\>\<emph\>Indexes\</emph\>\</link\> or \<emph\>Format - Spacing - Category - \</emph\>\<link href=\"text/smath/01/05030000.xhp\" name=\"Limits\"\>\<emph\>Limits\</emph\>\</link\>. Additional basic information about indexes is given elsewhere in the \<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Help\"\>Help\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03090300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155956				0	ca-XR	When you type information manually in the Commands window, note that a number of operators require spaces for correct structure. This is especially true when your operators are supplied with values instead of placeholders, for example, lim a_{n}=a.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02110000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Previous Error				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02110000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>error search; previous error\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02110000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/02110000.xhp\" name=\"Previous Error\"\>Previous Error\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02110000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	ca-XR	\<ahelp hid=\"SID_PREVERR\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Moves the cursor to the previous error (moving left).\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Previous Marker				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153770				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>markers; previous\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>placeholders; previous marker\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/02090000.xhp\" name=\"Previous Marker\"\>Previous Marker\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252				0	ca-XR	\<ahelp hid=\"SID_PREVMARK\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Moves the cursor to the previous marker (to the left).\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\02090000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148488				0	ca-XR	"Markers" are placeholders. They take the form of <?> in the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Indexes and Exponents				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150746				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>indexes and exponents in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>exponents and indexes in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150746				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/smath/01/03091200.xhp\" name=\"Indexes and Exponents\"\>Indexes and Exponents\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153730				0	ca-XR	Here, you will find basic information about indexes and exponents in \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\>. You can try the examples described here to help you understand the details discussed. (The quotation marks in this text are for emphasis purposes only and are not part of the examples.)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149884				0	ca-XR	The index and exponent for a character are displayed one on top of the other, left-justified to the base character. For example, type \<emph\>a_2^3\</emph\> or \<emph\>a^3_2\</emph\>. This can be in any order. Instead of \<emph\>'_'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'^'\</emph\>, you can use \<emph\>'sub'\</emph\> and \<emph\>'sup'\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148387				0	ca-XR	However, it is no longer possible to use the following patterns				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149029				0	ca-XR	a_2_3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155985				0	ca-XR	a^2^3				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153923				0	ca-XR	a_2^3_4				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153724				0	ca-XR	Each sub-/superscript position of a base character can only be used once. You must use brackets to indicate the desired result. The following examples illustrate this				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185				0	ca-XR	a_{2_3}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151272				0	ca-XR	a^{2^3}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156316				0	ca-XR	a_2^{3_4}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145207				0	ca-XR	a_{2^3}^{4_5}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151173	13			0	ca-XR	Unlike other formula editors where "\<emph\>_\</emph\>" and " \<emph\>^\</emph\> " only refer to the next character ("a_24" refers only to the "2"), $[officename] Math refers to the entire number(s)/name(s)/text. If you want to put superscripts and subscripts in sequence, the expression can be written as follows: a_2{}^3 or a^3{}_2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260				0	ca-XR	To write tensors, \<emph\>$[officename] Math\</emph\> provides several options. In addition to the notation "R_i{}^{jk}{}_l", common in other applications, additional notations can be used, namely "R_i{}^jk{}_l" and "{{R_i}^jk}_l."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147516				0	ca-XR	Super- and subscripts to the left of the base character can also be right-justified. To do this, the new commands "lsub" and "lsup" are used. Both commands have the same effect as "sub" and "sup", except that they are left of the base character. See also "a lsub 2 lsup 3."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154276				0	ca-XR	The rules governing unambiguity and the necessity of using brackets remain the same. In principle, this can be achieved with \<emph\>{}_2^3 a\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152961				0	ca-XR	The commands "sub" and "sup" are also available as "rsub" and "rsup".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3158437				0	ca-XR	Using the "csub" and "csup" commands, you can write super- and subscripts directly above or below a character. An example is "a csub y csup x". Combinations of indexes and exponents together are also possible: "abc_1^2 lsub 3 lsup 4 csub 55555 csup 66666."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154570				0	ca-XR	Super- and subscripts can be attached to most unary and binary operators. Two examples: "a div_2 b a<csub n b +_2 h" and "a toward csub f b x toward csup f y."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\01\03091200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155904				0	ca-XR	Be sure to also enter all spaces between characters when entering these examples into the \<emph\>Commands\</emph\> window.				20200411 15:09:53
