helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400				0	ca-XR	Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3150752				0	ca-XR	To choose the details proceed as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	ca-XR	Select the sheet you want to print.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042				0	ca-XR	Choose \<emph\>Format - Page\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3147340	13			0	ca-XR	The command is not visible if the sheet was opened with write protection on. In that case, click the \<emph\>Edit File \</emph\>icon on the \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> Bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3146916	14			0	ca-XR	Select the \<emph\>Sheet\</emph\> tab. In the \<emph\>Print \</emph\>area mark the details to be printed and click OK.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3145789				0	ca-XR	Print the document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3150345				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/03100000.xhp\" name=\"View - Page Break Preview\"\>View - Page Break Preview\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Calculating With Dates and Times				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146120				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>dates; in cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>times; in cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cells;date and time formats\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>current date and time values\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146120				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"calc_date\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_date.xhp\" name=\"Calculating With Dates and Times\"\>Calculating With Dates and Times\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3154320				0	ca-XR	In $[officename] Calc, you can perform calculations with current date and time values. As an example, to find out exactly how old you are in seconds or hours, follow the following steps:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150750				0	ca-XR	In a spreadsheet, enter your birthday in cell A1.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3145642	14			0	ca-XR	Enter the following formula in cell A3: \<item type=\"literal\"\>=NOW()-A1\</item\>            				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149020				0	ca-XR	After pressing the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key you will see the result in date format. Since the result should show the difference between two dates as a number of days, you must format cell A3 as a number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	ca-XR	Place the cursor in cell A3, right-click to open a context menu and choose \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	ca-XR	The \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format Cells\</item\> dialog appears. On the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Numbers\</item\> tab, the "Number" category will appear already highlighted. The format is set to "General", which causes the result of a calculation containing date entries to be displayed as a date. To display the result as a number, set the number format to "-1,234" and close the dialog with the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>OK\</item\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001				0	ca-XR	The number of days between today's date and the specified date is displayed in cell A3.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304				0	ca-XR	Experiment with some additional formulas: in A4 enter =A3*24 to calculate the hours, in A5 enter =A4*60 for the minutes, and in A6 enter =A5*60 for seconds. Press the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key after each formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149207				0	ca-XR	The time since your date of birth will be calculated and displayed in the various units. The values are calculated as of the exact moment when you entered the last formula and pressed the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key. This value is not automatically updated, although "Now" continuously changes. In the \<emph\>Tools\</emph\> menu, the menu item \<emph\>Cell Contents - AutoCalculate\</emph\> is normally active; however, automatic calculation does not apply to the function NOW. This ensures that your computer is not solely occupied with updating the sheet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Writing Multi-line Text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>text in cells; multi-line\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; text breaks\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>breaks in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>multi-line text in cells\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154346				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"text_wrap\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/text_wrap.xhp\" name=\"Writing Multi-line Text\"\>Writing Multi-line Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280				0	ca-XR	Pressing the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Enter keys inserts a manual line break. This shortcut only works directly in the cell, not in the input line.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3153142				0	ca-XR	If you want the text to automatically break at the right border of the cell, proceed as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	ca-XR	Select all the cells where you want the text to break at the right border.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	ca-XR	In \<emph\>Format - Cells - Alignment\</emph\>, mark the \<emph\>Wrap text automatically\</emph\> option and click OK.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_wrap.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05020000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cell\"\>Format - Cell\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Copying Formulas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151113				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>formulas; copying and pasting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying; formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>pasting;formulas\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151113				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"formula_copy\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formula_copy.xhp\" name=\"Copying Formulas\"\>Copying Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424				0	ca-XR	There are various ways to copy a formula. One suggested method is:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3150439				0	ca-XR	Select the cell containing the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	ca-XR	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Copy\</emph\>, or press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+C to copy it.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3159155				0	ca-XR	Select the cell into which you want the formula to be copied.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728				0	ca-XR	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste\</emph\>, or press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+V. The formula will be positioned in the new cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3149961				0	ca-XR	If you want to copy a formula into multiple cells, there is a quick and easy way to copy into adjacent cell areas:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400				0	ca-XR	Select the cell containing the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3154018				0	ca-XR	Position the mouse on the bottom right of the highlighted border of the cell, and continue holding down the mouse button until the pointer changes to a cross-hair symbol.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	ca-XR	With the mouse button pressed, drag it down or to the right over all the cells into which you want to copy the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3153714				0	ca-XR	When you release the mouse button, the formula will be copied into the cells and automatically adjusted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3156385				0	ca-XR	If you do not want values and texts to be automatically adjusted, then hold down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> key when dragging. Formulas, however, are always adjusted accordingly.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Applying Filters				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153896				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>filters; applying/removing\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>rows;removing/redisplaying with filters\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>removing;filters\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153896				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"filters\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/filters.xhp\" name=\"Applying Filters\"\>Applying Filters\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	par_id3150869				0	ca-XR	Filters and advanced filters allow you to work on certain filtered rows (records) of a data range. In the spreadsheets in $[officename] there are various possibilities for applying filters.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	par_id3155131				0	ca-XR	One use for the \<emph\>AutoFilter\</emph\> function is to quickly restrict the display to records with identical entries in a data field.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	ca-XR	In the \<emph\>Standard Filter\</emph\> dialog, you can also define ranges which contain the values in particular data fields. You can use the standard filter to connect the conditions with either a logical AND or a logical OR operator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	ca-XR	The \<emph\>Advanced filter\</emph\> allows up to a total of eight filter conditions. With advanced filters you enter the conditions directly into the sheet.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	par_id9384746				0	ca-XR	To remove a filter, so that you see all cells again, click inside the area where the filter was applied, then choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Data - Filter - Remove Filter\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	par_idN10663				0	ca-XR	When you select multiple rows from an area where a filter was applied, then this selection can include rows that are visible and rows that are hidden by the filter. If you then apply formatting, or delete the selected rows, this action then applies only to the visible rows. The hidden rows are not affected. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\filters.xhp	0	help	par_id218817				0	ca-XR	This is the opposite to rows that you have hidden manually by the \<emph\>Format - Rows - Hide Rows\</emph\> command. Manually hidden rows are deleted when you delete a selection that contains them.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Formatting Numbers as Text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145068				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>numbers;entering as text\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>text formats; for numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formats; numbers as text\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell formats; text/numbers\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formatting;numbers as text\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145068				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"text_numbers\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/text_numbers.xhp\" name=\"Formatting Numbers as Text\"\>Formatting Numbers as Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280				0	ca-XR	You can format numbers as text in $[officename] Calc. Open the context menu of a cell or range of cells and choose \<emph\>Format Cells - Numbers\</emph\>, then select "Text" from the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> list. Any numbers subsequently entered into the formatted range are interpreted as text. The display of these "numbers" is left-justified, just as with other text.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3149377				0	ca-XR	If you have already entered normal numbers in cells and have afterwards changed the format of the cells to "Text", the numbers will remain normal numbers. They will not be converted. Only numbers entered afterwards, or numbers which are then edited, will become text numbers.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3144765				0	ca-XR	If you decide to enter a number directly as text, enter an apostrophe (') first. For example, for years in column headings, you can enter '1999, '2000 and '2001. The apostrophe is not visible in the cell, it only indicates that the entry is to be recognized as a text. This is useful if, for example, you enter a telephone number or postal code that begins with a zero (0), because a zero (0) at the start of a sequence of digits is removed in normal number formats.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\text_numbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells - Numbers\"\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Entering Formulas 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150868				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>formula bar; input line\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>input line in formula bar\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; inputting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;formulas\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150868				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"formula_enter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formula_enter.xhp\" name=\"Entering Formulas\"\>Entering Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id6848353				0	ca-XR	You can enter formulas in several ways: using the icons, or by typing on the keyboard, or by a mixture of both methods.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145364				0	ca-XR	Click the cell in which you want to enter the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3150012				0	ca-XR	Click the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> icon on the Formula Bar.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441				0	ca-XR	You will now see an equals sign in the input line and you can begin to input the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153726				0	ca-XR	After entering the required values, press Enter or click \<emph\>Accept\</emph\> to insert the result in the active cell. If you want to clear your entry in the input line, press Escape or click \<emph\>Cancel\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3147394				0	ca-XR	You can also enter the values and the formulas directly into the cells, even if you cannot see an input cursor. Formulas must always begin with an equals sign.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id4206976				0	ca-XR	You can also press the + or - key on the numerical keyboard to start a formula. NumLock must be "on". For example, press the following keys in succession:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id1836909				0	ca-XR	+ 5 0 - 8 Enter				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id8171330				0	ca-XR	You see the result \<item type=\"literal\"\>42\</item\> in the cell. The cell contains the formula \<item type=\"literal\"\>=+50-8\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3155764				0	ca-XR	If you are editing a formula with references, the references and the associated cells will be highlighted with the same color. You can now resize the reference border using the mouse, and the reference in the formula displayed in the input line also changes. \<emph\>Show references in color\</emph\> can be deactivated under \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01060300.xhp\" name=\"Spreadsheet - View\"\>%PRODUCTNAME Calc - View\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3149210	7			0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"tip\"\>If you would like to view the calculation of individual elements of a formula, select the respective elements and press F9. For example, in the formula =SUM(A1:B12)*SUM(C1:D12) select the section SUM(C1:D12) and press F9 to view the subtotal for this area. \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304				0	ca-XR	If an error occurs when creating the formula, an \<link href=\"text/scalc/05/02140000.xhp\" name=\"error message\"\>error message\</link\> appears in the active cell.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3152993				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/scalc/main0206.xhp\" name=\"Formula bar\"\>Formula bar\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Recognizing Names as Addressing				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	bm_id3148797				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>automatic addressing in tables\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>natural language addressing\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulas; using row/column labels\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>text in cells; as addressing\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>addressing; automatic\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>name recognition on/off\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>row headers;using in formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>column headers;using in formulas\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>columns; finding labels automatically\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>rows; finding labels automatically\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>recognizing; column and row labels\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148797				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"address_auto\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/address_auto.xhp\" name=\"Recognizing Names as Addressing\"\>Recognizing Names as Addressing\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3152597				0	ca-XR	You can use cells with text to refer to the rows or to the columns that contain the cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283				0	ca-XR	\<image id=\"img_id3154942\" src=\"res/helpimg/names_as_addressing.png\" width=\"2.1291in\" height=\"0.8709in\" localize=\"true\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3154942\"\>Example spreadsheet\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3154512				0	ca-XR	In the example spreadsheet, you can use the string \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Column One'\</item\> in a formula to refer to the cell range \<item type=\"literal\"\>B3\</item\> to \<item type=\"literal\"\>B5\</item\>, or \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Column Two'\</item\> for the cell range \<item type=\"literal\"\>C2\</item\> to \<item type=\"literal\"\>C5\</item\>. You can also use \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Row One'\</item\> for the cell range \<item type=\"literal\"\>B3\</item\> to \<item type=\"literal\"\>D3\</item\>, or \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Row Two'\</item\> for the cell range \<item type=\"literal\"\>B4\</item\> to \<item type=\"literal\"\>D4\</item\>. The result of a formula that uses a cell name, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>SUM('Column One')\</item\>, is 600.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	ca-XR	This function is active by default. To turn this function off, choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Calc - Calculate\</emph\> and clear the \<emph\>Automatically find column and row labels\</emph\> check box.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\address_auto.xhp	0	help	par_id3149210				0	ca-XR	If you want a name to be automatically recognized by Calc, the name must start with a letter and be composed of alphanumeric characters. If you enter the name in the formula yourself, enclose the name in single quotation marks ('). If a single quotation mark appears in a name, you must enter a backslash in front of the quotation mark, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>'Harry\\'s Bar'.\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Cells in Currency Format				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156329				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>currency formats; spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; currency formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>international currency formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formats; currency formats in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>currencies; default currencies\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defaults;currency formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing;currency formats\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156329				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"currency_format\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/currency_format.xhp\" name=\"Cells in Currency Format\"\>Cells in Currency Format\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968				0	ca-XR	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc you can give numbers any currency format. When you click the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Currency\</item\> icon \<image id=\"img_id3150791\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_currencyfield.png\" width=\"0.1665in\" height=\"0.1665in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150791\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Formatting\</item\> bar to format a number, the cell is given the default currency format set under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Language Settings - Languages\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	ca-XR	Exchanging of \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc documents can lead to misunderstandings, if your \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc document is loaded by a user who uses a different default currency format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3156442				0	ca-XR	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Calc you can define that a number that you have formatted as "1,234.50 €", still remains in euros in another country and does not become dollars.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3151075				0	ca-XR	You can change the currency format in the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format Cells\</item\> dialog (choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</item\> tab) by two country settings. In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Language\</item\> combo box select the basic setting for decimal and thousands separators. In the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format\</item\> list box you can select the currency symbol and its position.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	ca-XR	For example, if the language is set to "Default" and you are using a german locale setting, the currency format will be "1.234,00 €". A point is used before the thousand digits and a comma before the decimal places. If you now select the subordinate currency format "$ English (US)" from the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format\</item\> list box , you will get the following format: "$ 1.234,00". As you can see, the separators have remained the same. Only the currency symbol has been changed and converted, but the underlying format of the notation remains the same as in the locale setting.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	ca-XR	If, under \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Language\</item\>, you convert the cells to "English (US)", the English-language locale setting is also transferred and the default currency format is now "$ 1,234.00".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\currency_format.xhp	0	help	par_id3154255				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Cells - Numbers\"\>Format - Cells - Numbers\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Entering Values				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150868				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>values; inserting in multiple cells\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>inserting;values\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>cell ranges;selecting for data entries\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>areas, see also cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3405255				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"cell_enter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_enter.xhp\"\>Entering Values\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7147129				0	ca-XR	Calc can simplify entering data and values into multiple cells. You can change some settings to conform to your preferences.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id5621509				0	ca-XR	To Enter Values Into a Range of Cells Manually				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id8200018				0	ca-XR	There are two features that assist you when you enter a block of data manually.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id1867427				0	ca-XR	Area Detection for New Rows				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7908871				0	ca-XR	In the row below a heading row, you can advance from one cell to the next with the Tab key. After you enter the value into the last cell in the current row, press Enter. Calc positions the cursor below the first cell of the current block.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id6196783				0	ca-XR	\<image id=\"img_id6473586\" src=\"res/helpimg/area1.png\" width=\"4.8335in\" height=\"1.5937in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id6473586\"\>area detection\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id8118839				0	ca-XR	In row 3, press Tab to advance from cell B3 to C3, D3, and E3. Then press Enter to advance to B4.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3583788				0	ca-XR	Area Selection				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id2011780				0	ca-XR	Use drag-and-drop to select the area where you want to input values. But start dragging from the last cell of the area and release the mouse button when you have selected the first cell. Now you can start to input values. Always press the Tab key to advance to the next cell. You will not leave the selected area.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id7044282				0	ca-XR	\<image id=\"img_id2811365\" src=\"res/helpimg/area2.png\" width=\"4.8335in\" height=\"1.5937in\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id2811365\"\>area selection\</alt\>\</image\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3232520				0	ca-XR	Select the area from E7 to B3. Now B3 is waiting for your input. Press Tab to advance to the next cell within the selected area.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id8950163				0	ca-XR	To Enter Values to a Range of Cells Automatically				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id633869				0	ca-XR	See \<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_series.xhp\"\>Automatically Filling in Data Based on Adjacent Cells\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Calculating Time Differences				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;time differences\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>time differences\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"calc_timevalues\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_timevalues.xhp\" name=\"Calculating Time Differences\"\>Calculating Time Differences\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3149263				0	ca-XR	If you want to calculate time differences, for example, the time between 23:30 and 01:10 in the same night, use the following formula:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3159153				0	ca-XR	=(B2<A2)+B2-A2				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3152598				0	ca-XR	The later time is B2 and the earlier time is A2. The result of the example is 01:40 or 1 hour and 40 minutes.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3145271				0	ca-XR	In the formula, an entire 24-hour day has a value of 1 and one hour has a value of 1/24. The logical value in parentheses is 0 or 1, corresponding to 0 or 24 hours. The result returned by the formula is automatically issued in time format due to the sequence of the operands.				20200411 15:09:53
