helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\03080000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154366				0	ca-XR	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:ViewValueHighlighting\"\>Displays cell contents in different colors, depending on type.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\03080000.xhp	0	help	par_id3125863				0	ca-XR	To remove the highlighting, unmark the menu entry.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\03080000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145785				0	ca-XR	Text cells are formatted in black, formulas in green, and number cells in blue, no matter how their display is formatted.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\03080000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153188				0	ca-XR	If this function is active, colors that you define in the document will not be displayed. When you deactivate the function, the user-defined colors are displayed again.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	tit				0	ca-XR	Text to Columns				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	bm_id8004394				0	ca-XR	\<bookmark_value\>text to columns\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	hd_id2300180				0	ca-XR	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/text2columns.xhp\"\>Text to Columns\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id655232				0	ca-XR	\<variable id=\"text2columns\"\>Opens the Text to Columns dialog, where you enter settings to expand the contents of selected cells to multiple cells. \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	hd_id9599597				0	ca-XR	To expand cell contents to multiple cells				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id2021546				0	ca-XR	You can expand cells that contain comma separated values (CSV) into multiple cells in the same row. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id2623981				0	ca-XR	For example, cell A1 contains the comma separated values \<item type=\"literal\"\>1,2,3,4\</item\>, and cell A2 contains the text \<item type=\"literal\"\>A,B,C,D\</item\>. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id7242042				0	ca-XR	Select the cell or cells that you want to expand.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id6999420				0	ca-XR	Choose \<emph\>Data - Text to Columns\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id6334116				0	ca-XR	You see the Text to Columns dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id9276406				0	ca-XR	Select the separator options. The preview shows how the current cell contents will be transformed into multiple cells.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id8523819				0	ca-XR	You can select a fixed width and then click the ruler on the preview to set cell breakup positions.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id1517380				0	ca-XR	You can select or enter separator characters to define the positions of breaking points. The separator characters are removed from the resulting cell contents.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\text2columns.xhp	0	help	par_id7110812				0	ca-XR	In the example, you select the comma as a delimiter character. Cells A1 and A2 will be expanded to four columns each. A1 contains 1, B1 contains 2, and so on.				20200411 15:09:53
