helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id9029206				0	ca-XR	You want roman page numbers running i, ii, iii, iv, and so on.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3032319				0	ca-XR	Double-click directly before the page number field. You see the \<emph\>Edit Fields\</emph\> dialog.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id9139378				0	ca-XR	Select a number format and click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	hd_id5051728				0	ca-XR	Using Different Page Number Styles				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id1558885				0	ca-XR	You need some pages with the roman numbering style, followed by the remaining pages in another style.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id1541184				0	ca-XR	In Writer, you will need different page styles. The first page style has a footer with a page number field formatted for roman numbers. The following page style has a footer with a page number field formatted in another look.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id18616				0	ca-XR	Both page styles must be separated by a page break. In Writer, you can have automatic page breaks and manually inserted page breaks.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id6138492				0	ca-XR	An \<emph\>automatic page break\</emph\> appears at the end of a page when the page style has a different "next style".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id4569231				0	ca-XR	For example, the "First Page" page style has "Default" as the next style. To see this, you may press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command+T\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>F11\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> to open the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Styles and Formatting\</item\> window, click the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Page Styles\</item\> icon, right-click the First Page entry. Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Modify\</item\> from the context menu. On the \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Organizer\</item\> tab, you can see the "next style".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id291451				0	ca-XR	A \<emph\>manually inserted page break\</emph\> can be applied without or with a change of page styles.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id3341776				0	ca-XR	If you just press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Ctrl+Enter\</item\>, you apply a page break without a change of styles.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id5947141				0	ca-XR	If you choose \<emph\>Insert - Manual break\</emph\>, you can insert a page break without or with a change of style or with a change of page number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id1911679				0	ca-XR	It depends on your document what is best: to use a manually inserted page break between page styles, or to use an automatic change. If you just need one title page with a different style than the other pages, you can use the automatic method:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	hd_id9364909				0	ca-XR	To Apply a Different Page Style to the First Page				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id4473403				0	ca-XR	Click into the first page of your document.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id4313791				0	ca-XR	Choose \<emph\>Format - Styles and Formatting\</emph\>. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id4331797				0	ca-XR	In the \<emph\>Styles and Formatting\</emph\> window, click the \<emph\>Page Styles\</emph\> icon.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id4191717				0	ca-XR	Double-click the "First Page" style.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id2318796				0	ca-XR	Now your title page has the style "First Page", and the next pages automatically have the "Default" style.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\guide\pagenumbers.xhp	0	help	par_id399182				0	ca-XR	You can now for example insert a footer for the "Default" page atyle only, or insert footers in both page styles, but with differently formatted page number fields.				20200411 15:09:53
